      <span id="rank-display" style="font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted);margin-left:auto">effective rank: —</span>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-collapse" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;margin-top:.6rem;font-size:.67rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#06b6d4;display:inline-block"></span>animals</span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#10b981;display:inline-block"></span>places</span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#8b5cf6;display:inline-block"></span>verbs</span>
      <span style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:.35rem"><span style="width:10px;height:10px;border-radius:50%;background:#f59e0b;display:inline-block"></span>abstracts</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  This is not a theoretical concern — it directly harms downstream performance. Collapsed representations sacrifice semantic expressiveness: once "cat" and "philosophy" are encoded in the same direction, the model can no longer separate them as distinct concepts in its computation. Ethayarajh (2019) linked anisotropy to degraded generalization, and the NITP authors confirm this empirically: models trained with standard NTP have consistently lower effective rank and higher token-pair cosine similarity than those trained with NITP.
</p>
<p>
  The fundamental question is: <em>why does NTP alone fail to supervise representation geometry?</em> The answer is architectural — it is baked into the gradient structure of the cross-entropy loss.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §02 -->
<section id="s-blindspot" data-code="blindspot">
<div class="section-no">02 — The Gradient Blind Spot</div>
<h2>NTP Constrains One Direction, Ignores d−1 Others</h2>
<p>
  Standard NTP minimizes the negative log-likelihood of the next token. At each position <em>t</em>, the final hidden state <em>h<sub>t</sub> ∈ ℝ<sup>d</sup></em> is multiplied by the unembedding matrix <em>W</em> to produce logits, and the cross-entropy loss pushes <em>h<sub>t</sub></em> to have a large dot product with the target token's embedding <em>w<sub>x<sub>t+1</sub></sub></em> while shrinking it for all others.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">NTP gradient intuition</div>
  ∂L<sub>NTP</sub>/∂<span class="math-var">h</span><sub>t</sub>  ≈  (<span class="math-var">p</span><sub>target</sub> − 1) · <span class="math-var">w</span><sub>x<sub>t+1</sub></sub>  +  Σ<sub>j≠target</sub> <span class="math-var">p</span><sub>j</sub> · <span class="math-var">w</span><sub>j</sub>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">≈ a vector in the span of the token embedding rows (rank ≪ d)</span>
</div>

<p>
  The critical observation: the gradient is always a weighted sum of embedding vectors <em>w<sub>j</sub></em>. If the embedding matrix has effective rank <em>r ≪ d</em> — which is typical, since most model dimensions are not meaningfully activated by most tokens — then the gradient only constrains <em>h<sub>t</sub></em> within an <em>r</em>-dimensional subspace. The remaining <em>d − r</em> dimensions are a <strong>null space</strong> with zero gradient signal.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">null-space.canvas — the gradient blind spot</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click "Show Drift" to see representations wander</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div style="display:flex;gap:.625rem;margin-bottom:.875rem">
      <button id="btn-drift" style="padding:.3rem .875rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid rgba(237,100,100,.4);background:rgba(237,100,100,.08);color:#f87171;transition:all .15s">▶ Show NTP Drift</button>
      <button id="btn-nitp-anchor" style="padding:.3rem .875rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid rgba(139,92,246,.4);background:rgba(139,92,246,.08);color:var(--purple-l);transition:all .15s">▶ Add NITP Anchor</button>
      <button id="btn-reset-drift" style="padding:.3rem .875rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);cursor:pointer;border:1px solid var(--border-hi);background:var(--surface);color:var(--muted);transition:all .15s">↺ Reset</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="canvas-drift" style="width:100%;height:260px;display:block;border-radius:6px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  In practice, this null space does not stay zero — representations fill it via unconstrained optimization dynamics. But instead of developing useful semantic structure, they drift into whichever low-energy configurations emerge from weight initialization and gradient noise. The result is the anisotropic collapse we measured: representations pile into a cone along the dimensions the embedding matrix happens to favor, ignoring the semantic structure a well-trained model should maintain.
</p>
<p>
  The fix requires a second loss that explicitly constrains the null space directions. NITP provides this by requiring <em>h<sub>t</sub></em> to align with a concrete target vector — not just to score high on a single token logit.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §03 -->
<section id="s-arch" data-code="arch">
<div class="section-no">03 — NITP Architecture</div>
<h2>Predict the Next Token's Meaning, Not Just Its Identity</h2>
<p>
  NITP adds one auxiliary objective to standard pre-training. For each position <em>t</em>, the model must not only predict which token comes next (NTP), but also predict the <em>representation</em> the next token will have — specifically, the shallow-layer hidden state at position <em>t+1</em>.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">architecture.svg — dual-supervision training</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="arch-wrap">
      <!-- Token row -->
      <div class="arch-row">
        <div class="arch-box tokens">x₁</div>
        <div class="arch-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="arch-box tokens">x₂</div>
        <div class="arch-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="arch-box tokens">x₃</div>
        <div class="arch-arrow" style="opacity:.3">···</div>
        <div class="arch-box tokens" style="border-color:var(--purple-l)">x<sub>t</sub></div>
        <div class="arch-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="arch-box tokens" style="border-color:var(--amber)">x<sub>t+1</sub></div>
      </div>
      <div class="arch-arrow-down">↓</div>
      <!-- Shared shallow layers -->
      <div class="arch-row">
        <div class="arch-spacer"></div>
        <div class="arch-box shallow" style="min-width:300px">Shallow Layers (k = 4 of 17)</div>
        <div class="arch-spacer"></div>
      </div>
      <!-- Two paths split -->
      <div style="display:flex;justify-content:center;gap:3rem;min-width:580px;margin:.15rem 0">
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:.2rem">
          <div style="font-size:.62rem;color:var(--purple-l);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">position t → h<sub>t</sub></div>
          <div class="arch-arrow-down">↓</div>
          <div class="arch-box deep">Deep Layers (L − k)</div>
          <div class="arch-arrow-down">↓</div>
          <div class="arch-box deep" style="font-size:.7rem">h<sub>t</sub> (final hidden state)</div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-top:.2rem">
            <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:.2rem">
              <div class="arch-arrow-down">↓</div>
              <div class="arch-box proj">Projector P</div>
              <div class="arch-arrow-down" style="color:var(--green)">↓ cosine</div>
              <div class="arch-box loss-nitp">L<sub>NITP</sub></div>
            </div>
            <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:.2rem">
              <div class="arch-arrow-down">↓</div>
              <div class="arch-box unembed">Unembedding W</div>
              <div class="arch-arrow-down" style="color:#f87171">↓ cross-entropy</div>
              <div class="arch-box loss-ntp">L<sub>NTP</sub></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:.2rem">
          <div style="font-size:.62rem;color:var(--amber);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">position t+1 → z<sub>t+1</sub></div>
          <div class="arch-arrow-down">↓</div>
          <div class="arch-box stopgrad">stop-gradient</div>
          <div class="arch-arrow-down">↓</div>
          <div class="arch-box implicit">z<sub>t+1</sub> (implicit token)</div>
          <div style="font-size:.62rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);text-align:center">target for<br>L<sub>NITP</sub></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="arch-note">
      L<sub>total</sub> = L<sub>NTP</sub> + λ · L<sub>NITP</sub> &nbsp;|&nbsp; λ = 1.0 &nbsp;|&nbsp; ~2% extra training FLOPs &nbsp;|&nbsp; zero inference cost
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  Three design choices make this work efficiently:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Self-supervised targets:</strong> <em>z<sub>t+1</sub></em> is extracted from the model's <em>own</em> shallow layers — no external encoder, no extra annotations. The targets are generated as a byproduct of the standard forward pass.</li>
  <li><strong>Stop-gradient:</strong> The shallow representation is treated as a fixed target. Gradients do not flow back through the shallow layers from the NITP loss, so the loss only updates the deep layers and the projector — not the target generator itself. This prevents mode collapse.</li>
  <li><strong>Cosine similarity loss:</strong> NITP minimizes <em>1 − cos(P(h<sub>t</sub>), z<sub>t+1</sub>)</em>, which enforces scale invariance. MSE amplifies the scale mismatch between deep and shallow representations, causing gradient spikes. Cosine loss is immune to this and converges stably (NITP loss ≈ 0.04 for the 9B MoE at convergence).</li>
</ul>
<p>
  The combined training objective <em>L<sub>total</sub> = L<sub>NTP</sub> + λ · L<sub>NITP</sub></em> with <em>λ = 1.0</em> adds only ~2% computational overhead during training, because the shallow representations are already computed in the forward pass and the NITP head is just a single MLP projector. At inference time, the projector is discarded — no overhead whatsoever.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §04 -->
<section id="s-anchors" data-code="anchors">
<div class="section-no">04 — Shallow Layers Are Semantic Anchors</div>
<h2>Semantic Richness Peaks Early, Degrades Deep</h2>
<p>
  Why use shallow layers as targets, rather than middle or deep layers? The answer lies in where semantic information lives in a transformer. Recent analysis (Skean et al., 2025; Liu et al., 2024c) reveals a consistent pattern: shallow layers act as powerful contextual encoders that resolve lexical ambiguity and preserve fine-grained semantic details. Deep layers, by contrast, progressively discard these details to focus on the discriminative features needed for next-token prediction.
</p>
<p>
  The intuition: deep layers are specialized for NTP. They have been shaped by the very objective we are trying to augment. Using them as supervision targets would simply add a second copy of the same signal — no new information. Shallow layers, however, encode richer semantics than the model's output head needs, making them ideal anchors.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">layer-depth.canvas — semantic richness vs layer depth (3B MoE)</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click a layer to see its ablation performance</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-layers" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block;border-radius:6px"></canvas>
    <div class="layer-btns" id="layer-btns">
      <!-- generated by JS -->
    </div>
    <div class="layer-stat" id="layer-stat">← select a layer above to see performance</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The authors confirm this with ablations: among three choices of target layer (shallow L4, middle L8, deep L14 out of 17 total), L4 achieves the best downstream performance. Furthermore, the optimal depth is consistently around 20% of total depth across model sizes — a stable heuristic.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-cyan">
  <strong>Theoretical grounding:</strong> The cosine similarity in the NITP Hessian has a positive-definiteness guarantee only when <em>s = cos(h<sub>t</sub>, z<sub>t+1</sub>) → 1</em> — that is, when the prediction target is actually achievable. Shallow targets, which encode recoverable next-token semantics, satisfy this: the NITP loss converges below 0.1 in practice. Arbitrary deep targets would remain at <em>s ≈ 0</em> and provide no curvature guarantee.
</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §05 -->
<section id="s-shift" data-code="shift">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Temporal Shift Matters</div>
<h2>Predicting Forward ≠ Aligning in Place</h2>
<p>
  The temporal shift in NITP — predicting position <em>t+1</em> from position <em>t</em> — is not an implementation detail. It is fundamental to the objective's effectiveness. The authors ablate this directly: what happens if, instead of predicting <em>z<sub>t+1</sub></em> from <em>h<sub>t</sub></em>, we simply align <em>h<sub>t</sub></em> with the shallow representation at the <em>same</em> position <em>t</em>?
</p>
<p>
  The result is striking. Same-position alignment (<em>t → t</em>) achieves a very low alignment loss — the dot product collapses to ~0.03 — yet downstream performance is dramatically worse than the baseline NTP. Meanwhile, next-token prediction (<em>t → t+1</em>) maintains a slightly higher alignment loss of ~0.08 but consistently outperforms NTP.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">temporal-shift.steps — why predicting forward works</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through the comparison</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="shift-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" id="shift-step-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Same-Position Alignment (t → t)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Align the deep hidden state h<sub>t</sub> with the shallow representation at the same position t. Alignment loss converges to ~0.03 — almost perfect alignment. But this is a trivial solution: the model can "cheat" by making deep and shallow layers encode the same thing. No predictive signal about the future is injected.</div>
          <div class="step-code">NITP loss ≈ 0.03 (very low)  |  Avg benchmark: 18.75%  ← worse than NTP (21.10%)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="shift-step-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Next-Token Prediction (t → t+1)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Predict the shallow representation at position t+1 from the deep state at position t. This forces the model to encode information about the upcoming context into its deep representations — exactly the semantic structure NTP supervision ignores. The prediction is harder (loss ≈ 0.08) but meaningful.</div>
          <div class="step-code">NITP loss ≈ 0.08  |  Avg benchmark: 23.58%  ← +2.48 pp over NTP</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="shift-step-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Why the Difference?</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Autoregressive temporal structure is key. NTP already forces the model to predict the next token's identity. NITP adds: predict the next token's semantic essence. Together, these two signals supervise both "what will come next" (NTP) and "what will that token mean in context" (NITP), leaving no degrees of freedom unaccounted for in the deep representation.</div>
          <div class="step-code">L_total = L_NTP + λ · L_NITP  |  both predict position t+1 from context up to t</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="shift-prev">← Prev</button>
      <button id="shift-next">Next →</button>
      <span class="step-stat" id="shift-prog">Step 1 of 3</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  This experiment also rules out the simpler explanation that NITP is just "representation regularization". A generic cosine regularization baseline — penalizing pairwise cosine similarity between hidden states to prevent anisotropy — shows no improvement over NTP. The gains require the autoregressive prediction structure, not just any geometric constraint on representations.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ §06 -->
