<p>
  The improvements scale with model size: average performance gains of +0.8 pp at 1.9B, +2.1 pp at 3B, and +2.7 pp at 9B. This scaling behavior is important — it suggests that as the representation space grows larger, standard NTP's null space grows too, and NITP's regularization becomes more valuable.
</p>
<p>
  Beyond raw accuracy, the authors evaluate learned representations as frozen sentence embeddings on 25 MTEB tasks. NITP improves 23 out of 25 tasks while preserving the same validation cross-entropy loss — confirming that the gains come from genuine improvements in representation quality, not from any trade-off with language modeling perplexity.
</p>
<div class="callout">
  <strong>The takeaway:</strong> NTP is necessary but not sufficient. It teaches a model what to predict, but not how to structure its internal representations. NITP closes this gap with a single, cheap auxiliary loss — no external encoders, no extra inference cost, no architectural changes. If you are pre-training an LLM from scratch, NITP is essentially a free upgrade.
</div>
</section>

